{"reference":"Job 36","book":"Job","chapter":36,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Elihu in the Book’s Debate","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Job 36 is part of Elihu’s extended speech, which begins only after Job and his three friends have stopped speaking. Elihu says that youth had restrained him from speaking, but he then gives four uninterrupted speeches before the divine answer. His place in the book remains debated: some readers see him as preparing the way for God’s response, while others see him as another speaker defending an inadequate explanation of suffering. His absence from the opening scene, the divine address, and the closing rebuke does not by itself settle that question.","confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_036_abfbab8ad9ffaa168e50f867"]},{"id":"block_2","text":"Later reflection can describe Job in terms of endurance or steadfastness, meaning persistence through suffering and the story’s eventual outcome. That description should not be read as a demand for silent endurance of abuse or as a prohibition against seeking escape and protection.","confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_job_036_2899d52cdb069eae22c37c23"]}]}],"generated_metadata":null}